WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.122
AI can now write proofs faster than mathematicians can read them.

2
00:00:04.172 --> 00:00:08.090
Terence Tao notes that the false starts behind a proof help others understand it,

3
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.311
and that AI tools are opaque about their own process.

4
00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:16.675
Mantis keeps the process. Every attempt, failure and fix is a record you can open.

5
00:00:16.795 --> 00:00:23.978
This paper tackles Hadwiger's conjecture: a network that needs k colors should shrink to k points that all touch.

6
00:00:24.158 --> 00:00:27.005
An earlier draft claimed the gap was at most eight.

7
00:00:27.075 --> 00:00:30.289
An audit caught an inequality used backwards.

8
00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:34.811
Rewrites cut the gap to two, and the dead end stays on the record.

9
00:00:35.011 --> 00:00:41.951
The proof rests on a computer check: fifty-six groups of points, and all 1,540 pairs touch.

10
00:00:42.101 --> 00:00:44.867
Swap two points, and the checker catches it.

11
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:50.839
Next, a century-old puzzle: the shortest fence that blocks every line through a square.

12
00:00:50.889 --> 00:00:58.031
One tempting rule said deleting a point from a shortest network saves at least its distance to the rest. A triangle breaks it.

13
00:00:58.181 --> 00:01:04.517
The paper proves two sharp bounds, backed by exact certificates. The square itself is still open.

14
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:09.539
Across fifteen papers, counterexamples, failed routes and fixes are kept and labeled.

15
00:01:09.939 --> 00:01:12.802
Mantis. Keep the work behind the result.
